# Test Trellis services

This example starts real NATS and Trellis, provisions the Orders service,
connects a generated caller, and checks the RPC response. It uses the contract
and handler from [the service walkthrough](/guides/write-a-service). No
pre-seeded database or fake transport responder is needed.

Trellis does not prescribe a service's other tests or testing framework. A real
runtime is useful here because the behavior being checked crosses bootstrap,
authority, transport, and generated caller/provider boundaries.

## Prerequisites

Install `@qlever-llc/trellis-test` and `@std/assert` in the example's Deno
project. Have matching `trellis-server` and `trellis` executables available. The
helper downloads pinned NATS binaries on first use, which requires network
access. Run `trellis generate` before the test.

## A complete test

Create `service_test.ts`:

```ts
import { assertEquals, assertMatch } from "@std/assert";
import { TrellisTestRuntime } from "@qlever-llc/trellis-test";
import { TrellisService } from "@qlever-llc/trellis/service/deno";
import { participant as provider } from "./.trellis/ts/participants/acme-orders-service/mod.ts";
import { participant as caller } from "./.trellis/ts/participants/acme-orders-caller/mod.ts";
import { createOrder } from "./service.ts";

Deno.test("orders caller invokes the real service", async () => {
  const runtime = await TrellisTestRuntime.start({
    trellis: {
      command: {
        cmd: Deno.env.get("TRELLIS_TEST_SERVER_BIN") ?? "trellis-server",
        args: ["--config", "{config}", "all"],
      },
    },
  });
  try {
    const identity = await runtime.registerService({
      name: "orders",
      contract: provider,
    });
    const service = await TrellisService.connect({
      participant: provider,
      name: "orders-service",
      trellisUrl: runtime.trellisUrl,
      identity,
      authorizationContextEphemeral: true,
      telemetry: false,
    }).orThrow();
    let exit: Promise<unknown> | undefined;
    try {
      await service.handleOrdersCreate(createOrder);
      exit = service.wait().catch((error: unknown) => error);
      const client = await runtime.connectClient({
        name: "caller",
        contract: caller,
      });
      const order = await client.ordersCreate({ customerId: "customer-1" })
        .orThrow();
      assertEquals(order.customerId, "customer-1");
      assertMatch(order.orderId, /^[0-9a-f-]{36}$/);
    } finally {
      await service.stop();
      const failure = await exit;
      if (failure) throw failure;
    }
  } finally {
    await runtime.stop();
  }
});
```

The explicit ephemeral authorization store belongs to this isolated test; the
production entrypoint uses a durable file store. The test helper tracks and
closes connected clients. The service is stopped before the runtime, even if an
assertion fails; lifecycle failures are rethrown rather than hidden.

## Run it

In a standalone service project with executables on `PATH`:

```sh
trellis generate
deno test -A service_test.ts
```

`TRELLIS_TEST_CLI_BIN` selects the CLI used by the test helper. This test's
`TRELLIS_TEST_SERVER_BIN` variable selects its explicit server command. For the
checked-in example, from the Trellis repository root after building both
binaries:

```sh
deno task -c docs/examples/orders/deno.json generate
TRELLIS_TEST_CLI_BIN="$PWD/rust/target/debug/trellis" \
TRELLIS_TEST_SERVER_BIN="$PWD/rust/target/debug/trellis-server" \
deno task -c docs/examples/orders/deno.json test
```

Expected result: one passing test, with all child processes stopped. A Rust
provider can be launched as a child of the same Deno runtime;
`TrellisTestRuntime` is not an exported Rust helper.

For deterministic application transaction behavior, test the actual adapter and
database instead of starting a network solely to check SQL. In particular,
outbox atomicity means both domain writes and event intents commit together, and
both disappear on rollback. That property is distinct from this RPC test.
